---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ProfilePage',
    name: 'About Ahsan Tariq',
    url: `${site.url}/about`,
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: site.name,
      jobTitle: 'Backend Engineer',
      url: site.url,
      image: `${site.url}/ahsan-tariq.jpg`,
      email: `mailto:${site.email}`,
      sameAs: [site.github, site.linkedin],
      knowsAbout: [
        'PostgreSQL',
        'Query optimisation',
        'Elasticsearch',
        'Algolia',
        'FastAPI',
        'Python',
        'Data pipelines',
        'Data engineering',
      ],
    },
  },
];

const skills = [
  { group: 'Languages', items: ['Python', 'SQL', 'Ruby'] },
  { group: 'Frameworks & APIs', items: ['FastAPI', 'Ruby on Rails', 'REST API design'] },
  { group: 'Data', items: ['PostgreSQL', 'Redis', 'Elasticsearch', 'Algolia'] },
  { group: 'Async & pipelines', items: ['Celery', 'Background workers', 'Retries & idempotency'] },
  {
    group: 'Engineering',
    items: [
      'Query optimisation',
      'Index design',
      'Transactional integrity',
      'Data modelling',
      'Testing',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="About — Ahsan Tariq, backend engineer"
  description="Backend engineer with 2.5 years across fintech, marketplaces, operations and legal tech. Why I am moving toward data engineering and machine learning, and what I want from the DEAI programme."
  ogType="profile"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container page-head page-head--split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Backend engineer, moving toward data engineering and machine learning.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I am Ahsan Tariq. For the last two and a half years I have been the person who makes the data
        layer behave — schemas, query plans, indexes, pipelines — on products other people's
        businesses run on. I now also lead a team and own product direction on one of them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="portrait">
      <img
        src="/ahsan-tariq.jpg"
        alt="Portrait of Ahsan Tariq"
        width="400"
        height="400"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <section class="container section--tight">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I started on the API side and drifted toward the part of the stack where correctness is
        actually decided: the schema, the query plan, the transaction boundary. That is where the
        interesting failures live. A feature can look finished in a demo and still be selling the
        same tee time twice, or showing a grant that no longer exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        At A'sTechware I worked across five client products — golf operations and payments, grants
        discovery, a restaurant directory and marketplace, and warehouse and construction
        operations. The visible features had nothing in common. The work underneath did: make the
        data correct under concurrency, make it fast at the size it actually reached, and make it
        explainable when somebody asks why a number changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Serva I lead the backend. It is the first product where the output is a legal document:
        a model proposes the intake, a human approves it, a server records what happened at the
        door, and an affidavit is assembled from that record and signed. Working on a system where
        a plausible guess is worse than a missing field changed how I think about AI features in
        general.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--plain">Experience</p>
        <h2>Where the work happened</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ul class="timeline">
      <li>
        <span class="timeline__when">Present ⟨start date⟩</span>
        <div>
          <p class="timeline__role">Lead Backend Engineer</p>
          <p class="timeline__org">Serva · Canada (remote)</p>
          <p class="timeline__body">
            Backend owner for a process-serving platform: the five-level record model, AI intake
            with per-field confidence and human approval, affidavit assembly from court PDF forms,
            and billing from logged attempts. Python, FastAPI, PostgreSQL, Redis, Celery.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline__when">2024 — present ⟨confirm⟩</span>
        <div>
          <p class="timeline__role">Backend Engineer</p>
          <p class="timeline__org">A'sTechware</p>
          <p class="timeline__body">
            Backend delivery across client products: Golfpay (tee times, memberships, payments),
            The Grants Hub (Algolia search, saved lists, subscriptions) and Warco (inventory ledger,
            job costing, reporting). Team lead on Restaurant Resource, owning product direction and
            the vendor and subscription model.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline__when">⟨dates⟩</span>
        <div>
          <p class="timeline__role">⟨Earlier role or internship⟩</p>
          <p class="timeline__org">⟨Company⟩</p>
          <p class="timeline__body">
            ⟨Add one or two lines if it supports the DEAI application — otherwise delete this row.⟩
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--plain">Toolkit</p>
        <h2>What I work with</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="skills">
      {skills.map((group) => (
        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>{group.group}</h3>
          <ul>
            {group.items.map((item) => <li class="tag">{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--plain">Motivation</p>
        <h2>Why data engineering, and why DEAI</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        DEAI's premise is that AI projects stall without rigorous data engineering. I have spent two
        and a half years on the other end of that sentence: building the pipelines, review gates and
        audit trails that a probabilistic feature needs before anyone will trust it with a payment
        or a court filing. What I have is practical and hard-won. What I do not have is the formal
        foundation underneath it — cost-based query optimisation as theory rather than instinct,
        data warehousing done properly, statistics, and the machine learning that I have so far only
        consumed from the outside.
      </p>
      <p>
        The two-year structure is the reason I am applying to this programme rather than a
        single-country master. Fundamentals at ULB and UPC, a summer school and a distributed Summer
        of Code, then a specialisation and a thesis with industry or a research lab — that is the
        sequence I would design for myself if I were allowed to.
      </p>
      <p>
        ⟨State your specialisation preference. Knowledge-Driven Data Management and Intelligence
        (TUW) is the closest fit to my indexing and retrieval work. Business, Economics and
        Financial Data Science (UniPD) would extend the payments side. Data and Intelligence for
        Heterogeneous Systems (UCBL) is where the marketplace and operations data would fit.⟩
      </p>
      <p>
        For a thesis I am drawn to evaluation and data quality for extraction pipelines: how to
        measure per-field accuracy on a labelled sample, how to choose a review threshold when a
        false positive and a false negative cost different amounts, and how to keep human
        corrections from quietly degrading the record over time. It is the problem I am currently
        solving by judgement, and I would like to solve it with method.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I would bring to a cohort is narrower and more specific than "industry experience":
        I know where AI features break in production, because I have had to build the retry, the
        gate and the audit trail around them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--plain">Education</p>
        <h2>⟨Degree and university⟩</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        ⟨BSc Computer Science, university, graduation year. Add thesis or final-year project if it
        is relevant, and the modules that match the DEAI prerequisites.⟩
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="container cta__grid">
      <div>
        <h2>Happy to go deeper on any of this.</h2>
        <p>
          If you are reviewing my DEAI application, the case studies are the fastest way into the
          technical side. Transcripts, references and the motivation letter carry the formal case —
          this site is a supplement to them, not a substitute.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-row cta__actions">
        <a class="btn" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>Email me</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={site.cv}>CV</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
